  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, I need to forecast the probability that by each of the given deadlines, someone will publish a complete solution to at least one of the Millennium Prize Problems (excluding Poincar\u00e9), and that AI played a significant role in providing a new argument or proof step. The user wants probabilities for each year: 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050.\n\nFirst, let me list the problems still open as of 2025: Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann Hypothesis, Yang-Mills. These are all extremely hard. They've been unsolved for decades. Solving them requires revolutionary insights.\n\nNext, the AI part. Current AI systems can help with pattern recognition, automation of complex calculations, and even some automated theorem proving, but none has solved a Millennium problem yet. The question is, how soon can AI contribute a crucial proof step in collaboration with humans?\n\nConsidering the timeline. By 2026 (just over a year from 2025), it's very unlikely. These problems are deeply challenging, and even if AI makes progress, it might take longer. Let's start with 2026 at 0.05 (5%)\u2014very low.\n\nBy 2030 (5 years), maybe 10%? Maybe some incremental collaborations between AI and humans, but completing a full proof in 5 years? Still low, but higher. Let's say 0.15.\n\n2035 (10 years). By then, AI could be more advanced. Maybe autonomous theorem proving has improved enough. Still, the problems are hard. Perhaps 0.3? Maybe 30% chance.\n\n2040 (15 years). More plausible. If AI systems are significantly more capable, perhaps in the range of 50-60%? Maybe 0.55?\n\n2050 (25 years). By then, it's possible that AI's role is much more integrated into mathematical research. Even if not all problems are solved, at least one might be. Maybe 70-80%? Let's go with 0.75.\n\nBut need to ensure probabilities are non-decreasing. Also, considering that the event might never happen. However, the instructions say to account for that possibility but still give probabilities as cumulative. So maybe the 2050 probability is around 0.75-0.8. Let me adjust the numbers to be non-decreasing and reasonable.\n\nSo the final JSON would be something like:\n\n\"2026\": 0.05, \"2030\": 0.15, \"2035\": 0.3, \"2040\": 0.5, \"2050\": 0.75\n\nThat's the progression, but maybe a bit more smoothing. For instance, maybe 0.05 in 2026, 0.1 in 2030. Wait, the user said to give one probability for each deadline, not separate per problem. Also, ensure the numbers are reasonable.\n\nGiven the time frames, perhaps 2026 is still too early. By 2030, maybe the probability is a bit higher. Maybe 0.1. Then 0.2 in 2035. 0.4 in 2040, and 0.7 in 2050.\n\nYes, that seems more plausible. So the final JSON would be:\n\n\"2026\": 0.05, \"2030\": 0.10, \"2035\": 0.25, \"2040\": 0.50, \"2050\": 0.75}\n\nAlternatively, maybe increase 2035 to 0.3 and 2040 to 0.55, and 2050 to 0.8. The key is to have them non-decreasing and considering AI's potential growth. Since the exact probabilities are uncertain, but the general idea is that the longer the time, the higher the chance, but not too high for earlier dates.\n\nFinal answer:\n{\"2026\": 0.05, \"2030\": 0.10, \"2035\": 0.25, \"2040\": 0.50, \"2050\": 0.75}\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0.05, \"2030\": 0.10, \"2035\": 0.25, \"2040\": 0.50, \"2050\": 0.75}",
